'''Corporació Nacional de Fabricants de Solvents, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|Corporació Nacional de Fabricants de Solvents}}), commonly known as '''CNFS''', is a Balboan commodity chemicals company and the country's leading manufacturer of {{w|solvent|aromatic and aliphatic solvents}}. Headquartered in the [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]] of [[Sant Cristòfor]], the company operates its main production facility at [[Badia Curta]] in [[Jala]] province, adjacent to [[CIEA]]'s refinery, which supplies the petroleum feedstocks from which CNFS's solvents are distilled. It is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] (SCX: CNFS).

CNFS is the only dedicated solvent manufacturer in Balboa and holds a near-monopoly in the domestic market. Its products — including {{w|benzene}}, {{w|toluene}}, and {{w|xylene}} (BTX) on the aromatic side, and {{w|hexane}}, {{w|heptane}}, {{w|mineral spirits}}, and {{w|VM&P naphtha}} on the aliphatic side — are used across the paints and coatings, adhesives, pharmaceuticals, oil and gas extraction, and printing ink industries. The company is a key node in the Balboan chemical supply chain, sourcing feedstock from [[CIEA]] and supplying solvents to [[Polímers de Balboa]] (PDB), [[Grup Químic Central]] (GQC), and other industrial customers.

== History ==

=== Founding (1971) ===

Mikkel Joan (1935–2015) worked as a distillation engineer at [[CIEA]]'s [[Badia Curta]] refinery throughout the 1960s. In this role, he observed that the refinery's catalytic reforming and crude distillation units produced significant quantities of aromatic and aliphatic byproduct streams that were being burned as {{w|fuel oil}} or exported as low-value products. At the time, Balboa imported most of its industrial solvents from [[Volisania]] and other Illypnian countries at premium prices, and no domestic manufacturer existed.

Puig left CIEA in 1970 and spent a year developing a business plan and securing financing from private investors and the Balboan government's industrial diversification programme. In 1971, he founded Corporació Nacional de Fabricants de Solvents with a small distillation unit at Badia Curta, adjacent to CIEA's refinery. The company initially produced basic aliphatic solvents — mineral spirits, hexane, and aliphatic naphtha — from refinery byproducts, supplying local paint and coating manufacturers.

=== Expansion into aromatics (1980s) ===

In the early 1980s, CNFS invested in a {{w|catalytic reforming}} unit and an aromatics extraction plant, allowing it to produce {{w|benzene}}, {{w|toluene}}, and {{w|xylene}} (collectively known as BTX) from CIEA's reformate streams. This expansion transformed CNFS from a small aliphatic solvent producer into a full-spectrum solvent manufacturer. The aromatic solvents commanded higher margins and opened new markets in chemical synthesis, pharmaceuticals, and printing inks.

[[CIEA]] acquired a 10% strategic stake in CNFS during this period, formalising the feedstock supply relationship and ensuring long-term access to refinery byproducts. The Puig family retained a controlling interest.

=== IPO and export growth (1996–present) ===

CNFS went public on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1996, raising capital to expand its production capacity and modernise its facilities. The IPO was well-received, reflecting investor confidence in the company's dominant domestic position and the growing demand for solvents in Abyala.

In the 2000s, CNFS began exporting to [[Potocsí]] and [[Tapuya]], benefiting from the [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]] on manufactured exports. The company established a distribution terminal at the [[Port de Portnou]] to handle solvent exports, which now account for approximately 30 per cent of revenue. Domestic sales account for the remaining 70 per cent.

Mikkel Joan retired in 2005 and was succeeded as chairman by his daughter, Marta Joan. He died in 2015. Under Marta Joan's leadership, the company has invested in environmental compliance, modernising its VOC emission controls and waste management systems.

== Products ==

CNFS's product portfolio is organised into two main categories:

=== Aromatic solvents ===

* '''Benzene''': Used as a chemical feedstock and industrial solvent. CNFS produces benzene from CIEA's catalytic reformate via {{w|extractive distillation}}.
* '''Toluene''': Used in paints, coatings, adhesives, and chemical synthesis. Toluene is CNFS's highest-volume aromatic product.
* '''Xylene (mixed isomers)''': Used in paints, coatings, and printing inks. CNFS produces mixed xylene and also separates it into ortho-, meta-, and para-xylene for specialised customers.
* '''Aromatic naphtha''': Used as a solvent and feedstock in industrial applications.

=== Aliphatic solvents ===

* '''Hexane''': Used in {{w|vegetable oil extraction}}, industrial cleaning, and as a chemical feedstock. CNFS's hexane is supplied to the agricultural processing sector and to [[Corporació Agrícola de l'Istme]] (CAI) for oilseed extraction.
* '''Heptane''': Used in pharmaceuticals and as a specialty solvent.
* '''Mineral spirits''': Used in paints, coatings, and cleaning products. Mineral spirits are CNFS's highest-volume aliphatic product and a staple of the Balboan construction and maintenance sectors.
* '''VM&P naphtha''': Used in paints and coatings as a fast-evaporating solvent.

=== Specialty solvent blends ===

CNFS also produces custom solvent blends for specific industries, including pharmaceutical-grade solvents, electronics cleaning solvents, and oilfield solvent blends for drilling fluid formulations.

== Operations ==

* '''Headquarters''': [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]], [[Sant Cristòfor]]
* '''Main production facility''': [[Badia Curta]], [[Jala]] province — adjacent to [[CIEA]]'s refinery, with direct pipeline access to refinery byproduct streams
* '''Distribution terminal''': [[Port de Portnou]] — solvent storage, packaging, and export handling
* '''Feedstock''': CIEA refinery byproducts (catalytic reformate, crude distillation fractions, {{w|naphtha}})
* '''Employees''': approximately 2,800 (2026)

The Badia Curta facility is the company's primary production site, with distillation columns, a catalytic reforming unit, an aromatics extraction plant, and solvent blending operations. The facility's co-location with CIEA's refinery provides a significant cost advantage, as feedstocks are delivered via direct pipeline rather than purchased on the open market. The Port de Portnou terminal handles packaging, domestic distribution, and export shipments.

CNFS's exports benefit from the [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]], which offsets the overvalued ID's cost penalty on manufactured goods exports. The company's principal export markets are [[Potocsí]] and [[Tapuya]], where it supplies solvents to paint manufacturers, oil and gas operators, and industrial customers.

== Ownership ==

CNFS is publicly traded on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]]. The ownership structure is:

* '''Puig family''': 18% — the founding family, represented by chairman Marta Puig i Vidal
* '''[[CIEA]]''': 10% — strategic stake held since the 1980s expansion, tied to the long-term feedstock supply agreement
* '''Public float''': approximately 72% — held by institutional and retail investors

The Puig family and CIEA together control 28% of shares, providing stability and ensuring continuity of the feedstock supply relationship.

